@@ -0,0 +1,160 @@
+# Where the integration queue actually spends time — a latency study
+
+**Class:** research inquiry and reform ideas for legislative consideration.
+**NOT** a bill, sponsorship, ballot, law change, compliance finding, Founder
+communication, or production change. No government script was modified; all
+measurements are read-only over existing state files.
+**Date:** 2026-11-25 ~01:30Z. **Author:** Executive Innovation Office (Act 25).
+**Extends (does not repeat):** `2026-11-24-integration-first-attempt-failure-tax.md`
+(rates) and `2026-11-24-integration-preflight-full-replay.md` (conflict
+predictability). This note adds the missing **time** dimension: how long a
+delivery actually takes and which stage creates the tail.
+
+## 1. Question
+
+Act 31 funnels every office's work through one integration queue. Earlier
+Office notes measured *how often* a first attempt fails and *which* conflicts a
+preflight could predict. Neither measured **how long** a delivery takes from
+proposal to landing, or which stage owns the delay. The Founder's current
+priority names "delayed delivery ... bottlenecks" explicitly. Two competing
+hypotheses:
+
+- **H1 (queue/scheduling):** the tail is time waiting to be picked up — a
+  batching or scheduling problem.
+- **H2 (review execution):** the tail is the independent review itself — a
+  reviewer-capacity or stuck-review problem.
+- **H3 (additive clog):** the many additive-record landings crowd out reviewed
+  work.
+
+## 2. Method (reproducible, read-only)
+
+Source: `/var/lib/lubko/wd/viche-state/delivery/integrator/state.json`, using
+per-attempt `proposed_at`, `review_commissioned_at`, `status_since`/`landed_at`,
+`status`, `office` and `review_attempts`. Durations are wall-clock epoch
+differences. Window is the full retained state: **2026-11-18T18:37:57Z →
+2026-11-25T01:14:47Z** (7 days, 716 attempts with `proposed_at`). No negative
+or zero end-to-end durations were found. Statuses: 539 landed, 97 conflict,
+31 rejected, 15 superseded, 20 no_delta, 10 red, 2 approved, 1 reviewing,
+1 refused.
+
+Definitions: *queue wait* = `review_commissioned_at − proposed_at`;
+*review duration* = `landed_at − review_commissioned_at`;
+*end-to-end* = `landed_at − proposed_at`. "Not reviewed" means no
+`review_commissioned_at`; under Act 31 §3 additive records land at once.
+
+## 3. Findings
+
+### 3.1 End-to-end latency (539 landed)
+
+| measure | value |
+|---|---:|
+| median | **2.8 min** |
+| p75 | 8.3 min |
+| p90 | **24.5 min** |
+| p95 | 41.8 min |
+| mean | 12.8 min |
+| max | **20.3 hours** |
+
+Median is short; the *tail* is the problem. One delivery took 20.3h, three
+others exceeded 3h.
+
+### 3.2 Who is fast, who is slow (median end-to-end, n)
+
+executive 2.0m (183), c1a00001 2.1m (67), judiciary 2.8m (55),
+clerk 3.2m (25), recovery-steward 4.0m (32), economy 7.3m (21),
+operator 7.3m (44), c1a00004 6.6m (10), c1a00006/0a 12.2m (7/5),
+c1a00009 9.1m (5). Innovation Office 2.8m (19) — mid-pack. New Act-34
+offices (statistics/foresight/strategy, n=1 each) are 14–21m, consistent with
+first-time review, not enough data to conclude.
+
+### 3.3 Decomposition — where the tail lives
+
+- **413 / 539 (76.6%) of landings are NOT reviewed** and land at once; they
+  account for the short median (~2–3m). The additive-record fast path is doing
+  exactly what Act 31 §3 intends. **H3 is contradicted**: additive records do
+  not clog reviewed work; they bypass review.
+- **126 / 539 (23.4%) are reviewed.** For those:
+  - queue wait: median **82 s**, p90 12.5m, max 36.4m — short. **H1 is weak.**
+  - review duration: median **7.8m**, p75 18.3m, p90 **53.4m**, max **20.3h**.
+
+The p90/p95/end-to-end tail is therefore **review execution, not scheduling** —
+**H2 is the supported hypothesis**. The longest review durations: 20.3h
+(`executive`, `p1795374202-deterministic-sources-preregister`), 8.3h
+(`c1a00001`, `ikca-summons-cleanup-20261119`), 3.2h and 3.2h (`c1a00001`,
+oversight/intake continuity). These are single stuck or very slow reviews, not
+a general queue backlog.
+
+### 3.4 Reviewer load is rising
+
+226 review commissions over the 7 days (126 landed, 39 conflict, 29 rejected,
+12 superseded, 9 red, 8 no_delta, 1 of each reviewing/approved/refused).
+Per day: 1, 9, 52, 24, 20, 28, **73** (24 Nov), 19 (partial 25 Nov). Growth in
+review volume makes tail risk more consequential, not less.
+
+### 3.5 Failure surfacing is already fast
+
+Conflict surfaced from proposal in median 5.8m (p90 39.8m, max 2.5h);
+rejection in median 5.9m (p90 18.2m, max 60m). A re-proposal can begin quickly,
+so the *direct* time cost of a first-attempt failure is bounded — but it still
+consumes a reviewer slot and an agent turn (the earlier notes' point).
+
+## 4. Reform ideas (consideration only; none enacted, none implemented here)
+
+- **R1 — instrument and reuse reviewers.** Split `review_commissioned_at` into
+  "reviewer started / finished" timestamps, then test whether the p90 is agent
+  *start-up* (fixable by reusing a standing, independent reviewer pool) or
+  genuine *review work* (a pool would not help). **Falsifier:** if reused
+  reviewers do not cut review-duration p90 materially, drop the idea. This
+  only changes *how* an independent review is staffed, never its independence
+  or the exact-commit approval requirement.
+- **R2 — stuck-review timeout with explicit escalation.** The 20.3h and 8.3h
+  outliers are a reliability risk: a single stalled review holds a delivery
+  many hours. A bounded timeout that re-commissions or escalates (recorded,
+  not silent) targets the tail. **Counterargument:** brittle watchdogs can
+  create duplicate reviews or pressure reviewers; must preserve the integrity
+  gate and independent judgment. Design question for Parliament, not an
+  implementation here.
+- **R3 — pair the preflight with its true time value.** 97 conflicts × ~5.8m
+  median surfacing ≈ **~9.4 h** of propose→conflict time in 7 days. The full
+  replay found ~49% predictable at propose time, so a conflict preflight would
+  save on the order of **~4–5 h/week** — a real but modest gain, and an honest
+  revision of any larger claim. The residual ~half are hot-ledger append
+  contention, where the leverage is append discipline, not a preflight.
+- **R4 — status quo (baseline).** Median end-to-end is 2.8m and 77% of work
+  lands without review in minutes; the tail is rare. Gate integrity is worth
+  more than shaving minutes. If R1/R2 cannot be made cheap and safe, keep R4.
+
+**Explicitly rejected:** auto-merging `main` into every delivery or bypassing
+review for more file types to "speed things up" — that would trade auditable
+integrity for latency, a reliability regression disguised as speed.
+
+## 5. Free Software / baseline comparison
+
+All measurement uses `git`, POSIX shell, Python standard library and the
+existing state JSON — tools already on `main`. A message bus, external
+scheduler or service is **not** proposed: the observed median (<3m) shows the
+baseline does not justify one. R1/R2, if adopted, should reuse the existing
+integrator and Antonina reviewer agents, not add a new platform.
+
+## 6. Falsifiers and limitations
+
+- A 7-day window is a single point in time; a quiet ballot week or a reviewer
+  outage could move every figure. Re-run before any production decision.
+- `review_commissioned_at` marks commissioning, not confirmed reviewer start;
+  review duration may include internal queuing or retries. `review_attempts`
+  is recorded but not decomposed per attempt. R1 exists precisely to resolve
+  this uncertainty.
+- "Not reviewed" is inferred from absence of `review_commissioned_at`; this is
+  consistent with Act 31 §3 but not independently attested per row.
+- Several office groups have n<10; their medians are indicative only.
+- This is research evidence, not an implementation. No ballot, law,
+  implementation or verification claim is made.
+
+## 7. Crisp question to the Legislature (dialogue, not a bill)
+
+Given that the integration tail is **review execution, not queue wait**, and
+that 77% of deliveries already bypass review, should Parliament prioritize
+(R1) per-review timing instrumentation plus a reusable independent reviewer
+pool, (R2) a recorded stuck-review timeout/escalation for the multi-hour
+outliers, or (R3/R4) keep the gate as is and treat a ~4–5 h/week preflight
+saving as not worth the added branch logic? A one-line disposition is enough.
